      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Since 1975 the Hinsdale Historical Society has guarded the village’s past, running the Roger & Ruth Anderson Architecture Center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hinsdalehistory.org/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Three historic sites—Hinsdale History Museum, Immanuel Hall, and the R.—are preserved by the Society as living landmarks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hinsdalehistory.org/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Memorial Building, finished in 1929, first housed the public library before the library and village offices swapped places in 1988.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hinsdale%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Metra’s BNSF Line stops at three Hinsdale stations—Highlands, Hinsdale, and West Hinsdale—linking the village to Chicago.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hinsdale%2C_Illinois",
